# SSH

## SSH With GitHub

- SSH allows me to authenticate with GitHub using an SSH key.

- Instead of repeatedly entering credentials, I can use a key pair.

- There are two important parts:

    Private key
    Public key

- I keep the private key secret.

- I can give GitHub my public key.

- I should NEVER share my private SSH key.

- An SSH remote might look like:

    git@github.com:username/project.git

- SSH is commonly used by developers who work with GitHub regularly.

---

# HTTPS VS SSH

## HTTPS and SSH

- GitHub repositories can commonly be accessed using HTTPS or SSH.

- HTTPS example:

    https://github.com/username/project.git

- SSH example:

    git@github.com:username/project.git

- HTTPS is simple and easy to understand.

- SSH is convenient once I have configured an SSH key.

- Both can be used to push and pull repositories.

